6  Roche Geometry and Binary Classification
6.1  Eclipsing subtypes (by light curve)
EA(Algol type):  detached
EB(βLyrae type):
semi-detached
EW(W UMa type):  contact
Figure 20:Roche-lobe geometry of the three eclipsing-binary subtypes.  The two lobes touch at the inner
Lagrange point.
phase
flux
EA: flat out-of-eclipse
phase
flux
EB: continuous variation, unequal
minima
phase
flux
EW: nearly equal minima
Figure 21:Light curves of the EA, EB and EW types.
Reflection effect.When a less luminous star faces a more luminous companion, the side of the
cooler star facing the hotter star is heated and receives and re-emits more flux, so a region of the
cooler star is brighter.  This changes the brightness outside eclipse and is called thereflection effect.
6.2  Detached, semi-detached and contact binaries
Detached binary:  no mass transfer by gravity takes place between the stars.
TheHill sphereis the region where a star’s gravity dominates, itssphere of influence.  The
speed needed to leave it is the escape velocity from the star’s potential,V=−GMm/r.
SupposeM
1
> M
2
andR
1
> R
2
.  IfM
2
swells, its radius grows but its mass does not, so its
sphere of influence stays the same; after some time the star fills it.  This limiting surface is the
Roche lobe.
IfM
2
grows larger than its sphere of influence, matter flows fromM
2
ontoM
1
and forms an
accretion disc.  The system is then asemi-detached binary.
When mass exchange is complete in both directions and the two stars behave as one common
envelope, the system is acontact binary.
